    "episode_id": 92729,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92729.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "What a cool representation of a great song. Can't take my eyes off you. That's my nephew, Des Dueron. That actually was when he was on the voice. He was a finalist on the voice and he is about out in the world and he's having a great musical career. As a matter of fact, he's got a big concert coming up tomorrow night in Nashville. Isn't it great that you have people from your hometown that really make it I'm always excited. I mean, in Shreveport, we've got so many people from here that just made it big. Entertainment, entertainment, entertainment, athletics, big business. It just proves everybody in this nation has the equal right to just go after it. And thankfully, many people have been successful achieving their dreams. And many of those people have brought a lot of that success back to us. And I'm so thankful for that. Well, do you want to just dive right into the bucket of madness that we awaken to every day? Do you do that? And where do we start if we're going to do that? Let me just throw some thoughts out there for you. And let's start it with this madness in New York. anti-Jewish, anti-Semitism, pro-Hamas, whatever you want to call it. It's all the same. It's built on hatred. So with the pro-Palestinian protest at these college campuses up in the north, these prove anything, listen closely. It's that our President, Joe Biden, neither has the fortitude nor sense to do what's right for the country. He could do this, folks, if he really wanted to. We're a unique country. Why? How? Well, it's because we allow for freedom of assembly, freedom of speech. It's a protected right under the U.S. Constitution. We all know that. still as college campuses across our nation continue to be in turmoil from the pro-Palestinian protesters shut down classes, block Jewish students and professors from even going into the buildings they're supposed to be in. You would expect something of this magnitude to be a cause for concern for not just Joe Biden but any other president. Yet when Biden weighed in on Monday after an Earth Day event in Northern Virginia on these pro-Palestinian demonstrations taking place, he showed he's more interested in feeding into the anti-American part of his base than he is doing the hard work that would unify the country. Remember his promises when he was running campaigning? I don't want to be the president of the Democrat party. I don't want to be the president of the Republican party. I want to be the president of everyone. He's sure in showing that right now. He said this Monday, I condemned the anti-Semitic protest. That's why I've set up a program to deal with that. I also condemn those who don't understand what's going on with the Palestinians. These protests, they're more than what we're seeing. They're more than just any Semitic. If Biden would take the time to watch any of the footage released from these events, he would see, just like we all see, people involved in chanting this, death to America, along with their genocidal rhetoric. Despite this, there still is that right to peacefully assemble. And that is the term in the Constitution. We have an unfettered right. Government cannot abridge our unfettered right to peacefully assemble. The Supreme Court has a broad view of what is considered a peaceful assembly. Still, we, the American people, are fed up with the idea that the President of the United States would in any way condone this type of behavior. He couldn't muster an ounce of political courage or sense to tell these entitled, elitist college students to stop what they're doing. Instead, you know what he did? He emboldened their continued harassment of the very people he claims he wants to protect. He's like a worm on a hook held by the far left wing of his party, incapable of breaking free from them. It's DOJ Department of Justice. They have no issue going after grandma's that are peacefully parading the Capitol on January 6, or Catholics praying outside abortion clinics. But it's apparently a bridge too far to do anything to stop the insanity up at Columbia. Columbia University and the Big Apple that calls the college to shut down in-person classes through the rest of the year, not to mention the protest on bridges across the country that disrupt the flow of traffic, prevent emergency crews from helping citizens in need, or their sit-ins at restaurants and coffee shops. Biden's telling the American people he wants the radical vote more than theirs. These militants who burn the American flag, threaten their innocent classmates, they're more important to them than any moderate or swing voter who watches these protests in disgust and fear. John Federman, Democrat Pennsylvania Senator John Federman. Gotta be honest, he is pretty much anything but a right-wing hero, but still, Big John is able to avoid compromising on his progressive agenda while also calling these protesters out for being a-holes, and he used the word. It's not appropriate or legal or helpful to advance your argument if you show up to a Starbucks with a bullhorn and start yelling at people. That didn't make you noble, it just makes you an a-hole. That was what Federman said. It's very American to protest and to do that in the appropriate way. He said, I absolutely support that. I'm not suggesting that you have to agree with my view, but it's just saying that it doesn't allow you to disrupt lives and to inflict those kinds of damages on people who are just trying to get on with their lives. Listen, yeah, this is all my opinion. It's a sad day in American politics when a man recovering from a stroke, John Federman, has a stronger backbone than does the US president. Biden had a chance to unify the country, but once again, it's happened over and over, but it's now happening again. He chooses to support the delusional anti-American left. But what else is there to be expected from a man who has done so much damage to the core values of this country, then to align himself with the people chanting, death to America. Those people he's supporting, that's what they're praying for. It's bad enough they want to eradicate every Israeli, every Jew, not just from the United States, but from every place on earth. We're supposed to be the place that we pray for and we push and support and look for ways to make everybody have an equal starting point. And we tell them all the time, African-American moms and dads, Hispanic moms and dads, they're leaving their homes all over, South America, Central America, rushing to be part of the United States. Why is that? Because we're the freest, the most just country on the planet that promises people equality of achievement or attempting to achieve. No guarantees. but everybody has the opportunity to do what's necessary that they're willing to do to create the best world they can possibly make for themselves and their families. And these people, they don't understand it. They certainly don't appreciate it. But sadly, even though they don't deserve it based on their actions, legally, They still have the same opportunity as everyone else. Wow, I just jumped right into this stuff today, didn't I? Well, we have so many other things to deal in with today. I don't know if you heard, but the Senate passed that foreign aid package yesterday. And I know we're not gonna go into all the details of what it includes. We all know it's money, money, money for Ukraine for a little bit of money for Israel, some for Taiwan, some for some other places. The big thing of course is Ukraine, but there's something really good that's buried in it. I don't know if you know about this, but it is. Democrat-controlled Senate, they voted to pass House Speaker Mike Johnson's foreign aid supplemental yesterday. It gives billions in aid to Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan. But there's something in it I think we all should like. It could trigger a piece, a part of this bill could trigger a ban on the social media app TikTok. Now this is a really big thing. Senate voted 79 to 18 to advance the legislation. Send the package to the president to sign today, which he will. The supplemental approved of sending 61 billion to Ukraine, 15 billion to Israel, an additional 9 billion in humanitarian aid, and 8 billion to Taiwan, among other provisions. It includes measures to put pressure on China and Iran. Now, nobody's been talking about this part of the bill, but it's very important. Pressure, think about it. Pressure on China and Iran. When has Joe Biden been willing to do that? Also included a provision to force TikTok's Communist Chinese Party link parent company, ByteDance, to sell the app. And they have nine months to continue operating in the United States. If they don't do that, TikTok will be banned in the United States. Oh, by the way, just like it's always been banned and is today from being used in China. Mitch McConnell, who I've lost a lot of respect for him in the last few years. He's the minority leader in the Senate. He said this, this is an extremely important day in the history of our country and of the free world. They're all watching, waiting to see what we would do. Tonight, of course, this was last night. Tonight, the Senate will send a clear message. History will record that even as allies and partners may have worried about the depth of our resolve, even as Moscow, Beijing, and Tehran grew more convinced that our influence had run its course. And even as loud voices here at home insisted on abandoning responsibilities of leadership, America stepped up and a Senate held firm. Well, that sounds almost presidential, doesn't it? Obviously somebody wrote that for Mitch. It didn't come out of his heart, I promise you. So Democrat Senators Jeff Merkley of Oregon, Peter Welch of Vermont, as well as independent Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont, they opposed the legislation. along with Republicans, John Barrasso of Wyoming, Marsha Blackburn of Tennessee, Mike Braun of Indiana, Ted Budd of North Carolina, Ted Cruz of Texas, Bill Haggard of Tennessee, Josh Hawley in Missouri, Ron Johnson of Wisconsin, Mike Lee of Utah, sent the alumis of Wyoming, Roger Marshall of Kansas, Marco Rubio of Florida, Eric Schmidt of Missouri, Rick Scott of Florida, and JD Vance of Ohio. Wait a minute. That's a lot of Republicans voting against it. Does that mean this bill is no good whatsoever? Oh, GOP senators, Rand Paul of Kentucky, Tim Scott of South Carolina, Tommy Tuberville of Alabama didn't vote. All other Democrat senators supported the bill. Mike Lee, Mike Lee of Utah had been calling on 41 Republican senators to block the package. Over on the other side of the Capitol, the GOP held House passed the legislation with several votes on Saturday. Speaker Johnson has faced backlash from conservative members over giving aid to Ukraine while not including border security provisions, with the majority of the Republican caucus opposing that funding. Republican Rep's Marjorie Taylor Greene of Georgia, Thomas Massey of Kentucky, Paul Gosar of Arizona, have been pushing a motion to vacate effort over the legislation. The Senate passed a similar package in early February that would have given $95 billion in AD Ukraine, Israel and Taiwan, while also including border security provisions that critics slammed for not being strict enough. And that's just a reminder, but It didn't make the cut. And so this all has just been hanging out there for some time. By the way, Biden said he's going to sign the bill at the epicenter ball of this. And just as we started the show, I just happened to pop up and see an email from a close friend of mine. If I said this person's name, you'd know who the person is. seen and heard quite a bit on this show and on our website. And it was a question, one sentence, do you still support Mike Johnson? Well, let me just do this. We're going to take our first break on the other side of this. Don't go anywhere because on the other side of this, you're going to hear from Mike Jo