  {
    "episode_id": 92729,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92729.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "But those of you that are just like me, we don't live in a big city. We don't live in New York. We don't live up on the East Coast or out west in Los Angeles, Seattle or even up or Midwest in Chicago. It's hard for us to relate to some of the craziness. In fact, pretty much all the craziness that are happening to the people that live and work and raise families or try to in these big cities, especially under the oppressive situation and circumstances that this administration has created. You put all of that in context, and maybe you can find a way to not be so negative. You know, it's hard to reconcile when we have such differing opinions And we interact with a lot of other people that don't share the same opinions that we have. It's really difficult to just keep up a straight plane with your mind and your thinking and not live emotionally with jagged up and downs that just will drive you crazy if you let them. We're supposed to be united around freedom and justice for all equal opportunity. the rule of law, a chance to speak our minds, not to be assaulted verbally or otherwise, and know that our government is going to keep us safe. Boy, that used to be the way it was most of the time, but it certainly isn't that way now. And so we have our government We have Congress. These are the people we send up there. We vote them in to office on both sides of the Capitol, the House and the Senate. And they supposedly, when they take those oaths of office and they take the jobs that we've asked them to take and do the things that they tell us they're going to do in their campaigning. We expect them to do just that, but then we find out, well, We can't quite get that done, but here's what we're going to try to do while we're doing this other thing that we didn't talk to you about. It's impossible to even comprehend a likelihood that 500 people could agree on one thing, sufficient. And then put that in the context, the landscape of how many thousands of such things come across their desk every month. How in the heck has this nation lasted this long? No question about it. We have a lot of issues on the plate right now. And most of those issues are not good. And we have a lot of people that are in total disagreement on many of those things, somehow, some way, We need to find real leaders in this country that will step up and not just talk a good game, but play a good game. And to do that, listen to what I'm about to tell you. And I'm not one of these people that will tell you what I'm about to say we must do. I was there in my thinking. three, four, five weeks ago, I wasn't. I was one of those dividers in chief. I was one of those people that said, hey, I'm conservative. I believe in pure conservatism. And I totally reject anything and everything. And anybody that doesn't agree with that. How can we possibly think a nation can exist, a free nation, in which we have 10 amendments to our Constitution that our forefathers gave to us after they passed the Constitution. These elements and these 10 amendments they passed are so critical to the freedoms in America that they put in place back then. They wanted to make sure any government that came into the United States voted in at any time in the future knew they could not take these rights away from us. So why should any individual or any group, what kind of group, Dan, maybe a political party, maybe a group of people in The House of Representatives are working in a presidential administration or in a Supreme Court building. Why should anybody think those people have a supreme right to unilaterally or even in consensus with each other, change the rules, change the laws in the Constitution? Now, you got that for free. And the reason I said those things, I want you to think about what Speaker Mike Johnson's going through right now and what he's been going through since he agreed to take the Speaker's gavel and lead the House of Representatives. By the way, in case you didn't know, the House of Representatives isn't, even though it's been a, for a few weeks, a few months, a Republican majority There are 435 members in the House of Representatives. That means pretty much 435 different opinions about pretty much everything, right? Have you ever tried to herd cats? Ever heard of anybody being successful, herding cats? But the one thing every speaker promises to do is follow the law. Make sure that the people are in what is represented in the house and in any legislative actions that they take. It's by the people. It's for the people and it's of the people. Speaker Mike Johnson yesterday with Howard Hewitt. Listen in.",
