  {
    "episode_id": 92729,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92729.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, we've got a delicate balance right now. I've got the smallest majority in US history, famously, everyone knows that now I have a one-load margin. So when you have such a slim margin, you know, there are actions and then there are reactions and reverberations from the actions. It's a very delicate balance. If I start kicking people off committees right now, it's likely that I cause more problems than I solve. And so what I'm trying to do every day is manage this team. You know, people ask me all the time, he was at a big event in Dallas last night. And they said, why do the Democrats seem to stick together more than the Republicans? They said, it's actually pretty easy. The explanation is this. The Democrats think and act like a union. They're collectivist. You know, they move and are heard. Many of them are not deeply principled or philosophical folks anyway. And so you can command them and they'll move social animals, the socialists, right? We, on the other hand, a rugged individualist, and we're deeply principled and philosophical, and it's difficult to get us to move in tandem sometimes. That's a blessing. I love that part, except when you have a one-vote margin. I work every day to get the team together to understand the stakes, I think they do. And I think we're going to get this job done, deliver it to the people. And you, I believe we're going to have an extraordinary election cycle in November. I think we're going to grow the House majority and make this job easier. We're going to win back to Senate, and I think Donald Trump's going back to the White House. We'll be able to turn this thing 180 degrees.",
