    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92729.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "are going on, and what needs to change and how to change it when it's time to change things. Mike Johnson, I know him personally. He's a good friend. He's very, very smart. I have issues with some of the choices he's made, but I'm not inside his head. And we all need to understand this. We are not privy to but about 2% of everything that goes into being a house speaker. That's the 2% that can be and is made public. 98% of what the job entails is classified. And if it's not classified, he doesn't have time to go out and discuss with the general public everything that goes into making the decisions that have to be made every day by anybody in the government, especially somebody at that particular level. Tremendous amount of responsibility. And it's a very weighty situation. In answer to the question that I told you, I got asked through an email at the beginning of this show, talking to me, this person said, do you still support Mike Johnson? And I replied with this, do you still despise Mike Johnson? And by the way, this person doesn't know Mike Johnson personally. The difference for me is I do. And there's a scripture in the Bible Philippians 3 and 10. Philippians is a short book of the Bible. It was written by the Apostle Paul. And Philippi was a church that he established. And the people in that church were having some issues, issues about a lot of things religiously. And you got to remember the background, they all were part of old time Judaism. And the rules and regulations are quite a bit different in many cases with Christianity. The Apostle Paul was a Christian. Church in Philippi, most of those people were Jewish, and because they were Jewish, they were part of Judaism. That's just the way it is. Sometimes people make choices based upon most of the time, in fact, what they think about the facts are. Sometimes We say this all the time here, just because you think something's right doesn't make it right, just because you think something's wrong doesn't mean it's wrong. That same thing applies in the case of what you and I think about Mike Johnson or anybody else. Paul said in that scripture, talking about a personal relationship with the Son of God, Jesus Christ, that I may know him. and the power of his resurrection, being raised from the dead, being made conformable to his death. What was that? Death before Jesus died and was resurrected, it was pretty much a benign thing. But the point that happened when Christ lived here, then was crucified, buried, resurrected, And in heaven and life, eternal life, God offers that same opportunity to us. Paul was lamenting that even though he had spent his life establishing Christian churches all over the Middle East, he said, I'm not sure, basically, this is what he meant. I'm not sure that I really know Jesus. And I want to know him better and not just know about him. So I'm challenging anybody that questions Mike Johnson. If you don't know him, if you know him, you got your opinion, you earned it. Honestly, that's great. But if you don't know him, don't draw all of your conclusions about his job based upon what you think through your emotions. simply because we don't have all the facts. of us have all the facts. I can't even say everything Mike Johnson is doing is right or everything Mike Johnson is doing wrong. And I don't think anybody that's not walking in his shoes can say that honestly. So how, especially if you're Christian, how do you handle this? Well, when I can't get an answer to a problem, I learned a long time ago, I grew up as a Christian, I am a Christian, I turn to the Bible. I look for answers there. What does the Bible say about our political leaders? Even back then when the Romans were controlling and ruling over the Jews, what were they told to do? Pray for those in authority over you. In other words, support them, respect the job, the position that they're in, and pray for them that they'll make the right choices. and then stop right there. What we do in America, because we have the unfettered white right to say anything, think anything, you know, that First Amendment thing, we can think that everybody, they got to listen to me. They got to agree with what I think. That's not the way it works. Life is based upon choices. And we all make our choices, but never, never hardly ever do we make them the same way that somebody else would make them facing the exact same circumstances. And we must honor that and accept that. Sometimes we're just going to disagree. But where this group of people right now, and I'm talking about people that I have personally identified with for many years, very conservative political people, good people, many of them Christians, most of them Christians. But I had to get on my knees and ask for forgiveness because I really got hacked off at night for what he's done. He let that money go. They went ahead and did that bill. And he promised it wouldn't even come to the floor to be considered if it did not include money to close the southern border. That wasn't in the bill. And so I immediately, I just jumped right on the negative part of this, like the person that sent me that email earlier, right before the beginning of the show, they're living there right now. They're drawing their conclusion from their own opinions without any knowledge of all that went into the reasoning that that happened. Mike will be on this show, hopefully sooner than later. And he will give us the explanation. And when he does, it's gonna be along something like this. We can't govern unless we put things in place that are necessary for the government to run. One thing that is essential, we can all say, is money. Millions of Americans live and work for the federal government. If that bill to fund the government wasn't completed and signed into law, there were millions of people that would not be able to live because they would not be getting paid. numbering among those, Medicare, Social Security, military, and that's just a few. Can you imagine if your paycheck just stopped overnight because of some people having a political battle? And that's just one little example. Enough of that. I hope you don't resent the fact that I pontificate every once in a while, but I believe everybody has a right to express opinions. You may disagree with mine. I may disagree with you, but in America, we're supposed to be able to share our thoughts without being afraid that bad things are going to happen to us just because we express our opinions. We're going to move over after a break. We're going to move over and we're going to get into what's going on in New York today. The Hush Money trial. And there's something about prosecutor, the one that's standing up and actually in court prosecuting this, that you probably don't know about. And you're going to shake your head when you find out. That's next.",
