he Social Security Administration now to understand why our numbers are so different. So let me pick it that just a little bit. This is Catherine Engelbrit talking now. So this is misinformation that has been published by the Social Security Administration based on their databases that they published week over week over week over week. So it's just not like there was a one time bounce up. This just now made its way into social media, but it was posted online has been since I don't even know when it started, but it's coming from a federal government agency. And that is a little curious. I have lots of other issues about this. It's a thousand front war that we're facing this cycle, but that issue alone, has the potential of changing the playing field in a way that it'll be hard to address because there's so little clarity around what to do if your roles in your voter registry and your elections are as open as the border of Texas. So this is just one state. just a paltry 1.7 million mysterious new voter registrations in three years. Well, there have been more than that that have come across the southern border. So would it be likely to even think and maybe check on to make sure these people that are registered, those 1.7 million, every one of them legally can vote legally, can vote. So we have this conversation now. There's so many things Trump related going on. We talk about, we ask this, we look, we listen, trial after trial, appeal after appeal. It's hard to keep up with it all. But Trump v US Supreme Court, Trump v the US, that's a case regarding presidential immunity. The Supreme Court is likely going to determine when experts says that presidents do have some amount of immunity, some, it doesn't say total immunity from wrongdoing. They're set to consider the court is arguably the highest profile cases of this term. tomorrow to determine whether Trump can claim presidential immunity against criminal charges brought by the Biden Justice Department, Jack Smith, and 