dance on where presidential immunity from criminal prosecution ends for actions taken while in the Oval Office, which could have a profound impact in the criminal cases against the former president. One of those experts, a guy that I think a lot about Jonathan Turley, who's a practicing criminal defense attorney, and he's a professor at George Washington University. He said the case is surrounded by rather steep constitutional cliffs. Here's what he said. This case may be rather maddening for the justices because it's surrounded by rather steep constitutional cliffs. If the court goes one way, a president has little protection in carrying out the duties of his office. If they turn the other way, he has a little accountability for the most serious criminal acts he may have committed. This is a court that tends to be incremental. incremental. That's what Turley said. They tend not to favor sweeping rulings, he said. Jonathan, I don't know about that. Overturning Roe v. Wade was a pretty sweeping ruling. The Justice Department argued in lower court that a president has virtually no immunity when he leaves office. And the lower court agreed. Turley says the justices could reject the lower court decision and send it back for a more nuanced approach on constitutional immunity. The justices, he said, may find that presidents do require immunity, even with regard to some criminal acts. He said adding that any remand would work significantly in the former president's favor on a tactical level. And what does that mean? Well, Turley explained that if the case were to be remanded back down to Judge Tanya Chetkin in the DC District Court, that process would make a trial before the November election almost impossible. Turley said there are both constitutional and tactical aspects to the ruling, but he thinks these justices are likely to look at this argument with an eye toward balancing these interests And if that's the case, they could come up with a different approach than the 