    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92729.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 142,
    "turn_text": "Hey, listen, don't go anywhere before the end of the show. I've got one thing I want to talk to you about, and then I'm going to let you. Have you ever wondered how presidents come to office and they don't seem to be very wealthy? Maybe they are wealthy, but then we wonder when they get through with that, how much are they worth then? I've got those numbers for you. We're gonna get into that in just a second on the way out of the show. Federal Trade Commission yesterday, they did something that is right down the alley of labor unions. They voted to ban nationwide companies from using non-compete agreements, non-compete agreements, prohibit employees from taking new jobs somewhere else in their industry. The Biden administration has pushed the FTC since 21 to make changes to its non-compete policies, which affect even low-level employees who are not entitled to trade secrets that they might take to another new employer. The FTC first proposed the idea in January last year claiming it could increase wages and expand career opportunities. The Democrat-led commission, here we go, voted in favor of the ban, three to two. which will go into effect August 21st of this year. Two Republicans on the commission voted against the ban claiming that the FTC does not have jurisdiction to create or enforce the rule. The ruling will be challenged by businesses and courts. Now, let me just bring this down to ground level. 32 years, I owned a very successful company. It wasn't a big company. I guess at the height of it, we had at one time, maybe 105 employees in the company, but it was a specialized service company, a medical billing company. And we spent a lot of time and money training people that came into the company, not just to do the processes we do, but to do them in the manner that we created that was different from any other company. They were our ideas and plans, and they were very effective for medical clients all over the nation. And so what we did when employees came in, they each signed a non-compete agreement, and it wasn't a forever agreement. It was for a couple of years, if they came, And they worked for us and they went through our process to learn these secrets, these ways that we were being effective. They couldn't work for a direct competitor of ours for two years. Now, we thought that was fair and we used it. Everybody that came in understood what it was for and what it meant and what would happen. We only had one former employee try to execute that. And I hated to do it because it was a good employee, someone I liked, but we were constantly having medical clients from around this region, even in our own city, that would come and try to pick off our employees and get them to come over to them, to our competitors. And she did just that. So we took the court, And I prayed about it and I thought about it. And before we got to the ugly stuff, I decided just to drop it. Business people might think I was stupid for doing that because a word would get out and people would say, you know, they're not going to enforce that non-compete agreement. Unions hate them. because they want their union people to work in any situation they want at any time. So that's out there, but I promise you, it will be tested. I told you before we went, if you're like me, you wondered what presidents in the past, the near past, what they made, what they were worth before they became president and how much they had when they left office. Here you go.",
